research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4324-    },
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4325-    "lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular": {
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4326-      "citations": [
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4327-        {
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4328-          "fact": "L1",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4329-          "source": "lem-pseudometric-from-a-normal-entourage-sequence",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4330-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json:4331:          "quote": "Given a decreasing symmetric sequence $(E_n)$ with $E_0=X\\times X$ and\n$E_{n+1}^{\\circ3}\\subseteq E_n$, there is a pseudometric $p$ on $X$ such that\n$$\nE_n\\subseteq\\{p\\le2^{-n}\\}\\subseteq E_{n-1}\n$$\nfor every $n\\ge1$. In particular, each set $\\{p<\\varepsilon\\}$ is an\nentourage, so $p$ is uniformly continuous for the original uniformity in the\nsense of [[def-gauge-of-pseudometrics]].",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4332-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4333-            "2.1",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4334-            "3.1"
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4335-          ]
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4336-        },
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4337-        {
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4338-          "fact": "L2",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4339-          "source": "def-completely-regular-and-tychonoff-spaces",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4340-          "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4341-          "quote": "**The same condition in the vocabulary of zero sets.** With $f$ as displayed,\n$C \\subseteq Z(f)$ and $x_0 \\in \\operatorname{coz}(f)$\n([[def-zero-sets-and-cozero-sets]]), so complete regularity says: for every\nclosed $C$ and every $x_0 \\notin C$ there is a continuous $f$ whose zero set\ncontains $C$ and whose cozero set contains $x_0$. In particular\n$\\operatorname{coz}(f)$ is an open set containing $x_0$ and disjoint from $C$;\nthat alone is weaker than regularity, and the passage from the function to two\n*disjoint* open sets is the next item.",
--
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4495-          "fact": "L2",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4496-          "source": "def-continuous-map-top",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4497-          "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4498-          "quote": "**The same condition with open sets only.** $f$ is continuous at $x$ if and only\nif for every open $V \\subseteq Y$ with $f(x) \\in V$ there is an open\n$U \\subseteq X$ with $x \\in U$ and $f[U] \\subseteq V$. Indeed, if $f$ is\ncontinuous at $x$ and $V$ is such an open set, then $V$ is a neighbourhood of\n$f(x)$, so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ and contains an open $U \\ni x$,\nwhich satisfies $f[U] \\subseteq V$. Conversely, given the displayed condition and\na neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$, fix open $V_0$ with $f(x) \\in V_0 \\subseteq V$ and\nthen open $U \\ni x$ with $f[U] \\subseteq V_0$; then $x \\in U \\subseteq f^{-1}[V_0] \\subseteq f^{-1}[V]$,\nso $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. Both forms are used below and are the\nsame statement written twice.",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4499-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4500-            "1.1"
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4501-          ]
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4502-        },
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4503-        {
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4504-          "fact": "L3",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json:4505:          "source": "def-gauge-of-pseudometrics",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4506-          "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4507-          "quote": "A **gauge of pseudometrics** on $X$ is a family $\\mathcal P$ of pseudometrics ([[def-metric-space]]). For finite $F\\subseteq\\mathcal P$ and $\\varepsilon>0$, put $E(F,\\varepsilon)=\\{(x,y):p(x,y)<\\varepsilon\\text{ for every }p\\in F\\}$. If $X\\ne\\varnothing$, these sets form a filter base and generate a uniformity ([[def-filter-base]], [[lem-filter-base-generates]]), called the uniformity **generated by $\\mathcal P$**.",
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.proof-contracts.json-4508-          "uses": [
